WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:02.302
- I think it's time we

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:04.971
the scientifically
challenged and concentrate

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:08.241
on one of science's
heroes, Tyler Bradt,

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:12.112
kayaker extraordinaire.

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:17.517
He wants to kayak over this,
Palouse Falls in Washington.

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:20.153
Thousands of cubic feet of
water pass over this fall

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.556
every second and drop 186
feet to the pool below.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:28.728
To consider kayaking
over this, he must either

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:32.232
be a few sandwiches
short of a picnic,

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:34.100
or a world record breaker.

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:34.901
We hope.

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:36.836
[music playing]

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:48.648
Oh.

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:51.151
There he is, the
new world record

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.420
holder of one of the
most dangerous stunts

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:54.454
I have ever seen.

00:00:58.525 --> 00:01:02.495
Needless to say, even if you do
have a 186-foot water feature,

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:05.131
don't try this at
home or anywhere else.

00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:07.500
Falling off a waterfall
is the easy part.

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:09.602
But as gravity initially
accelerates you down

00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:12.238
at 32 feet per
second per second,

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:16.409
it's surviving that's
the tricky bit.

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:19.546
Our kayaker must go over the
falls at the correct speed

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:22.282
to give gravity just
enough time to generate

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:26.019
the angular velocity needed to
rotate the kayak 90 degrees.

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:31.825
This angle minimizes
hydrodynamic drag,

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:34.294
so he experiences
less impact force

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.462
and cuts through the water.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:41.267
It's also worth noting
that a kayak's stability is

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:43.303
dependent on keeping
the center of mass

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:45.538
in line with the
center of buoyancy,

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:47.373
which is in the middle
of a submerged volume.

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:50.510
Otherwise.

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:54.614
OK.

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:57.016
Science-taught, let's see if
our team of want-to-be record

00:01:57.083 --> 00:02:00.220
breakers have learned.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:03.890
He's practicing the science with
a man-made kind of waterfall,

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:09.028
but the law of
gravity is the same.

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:11.297
The thin layer of water
acting as a lubricant

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:14.367
combined with the steep angle
allows gravity to do its thing.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.472
It's not quite the
32 feet per second

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:22.141
per second of a
waterfall freefall,

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:24.511
but he's only practicing.

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:31.551
On to angular velocity, or
in this case, the lack of it.

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:34.387
Launching too fast meant he
gained very little angular

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:37.891
velocity, hitting H2O
at a terrible angle,

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:41.928
thereby maximizing
hydrodynamic drag.

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:45.798
Which is bad, as it brought him
to an almost immediate and very

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:46.566
painful stop.

00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:53.239
Waterfall.

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:55.408
Check.

00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:56.676
Angular velocity.

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:58.478
Check.

00:02:58.545 --> 00:02:59.612
Stability.

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:02.515
Check, ish.

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:05.552
It's an upside down check,
but technically he still got

00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:07.687
himself into a stable position.

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:09.289
It's just a shame
his center of mass

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:11.558
is directly below his
center of buoyancy,

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:15.995
as his head is the thing
he uses to breathe.

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:17.463
Cheering without
acknowledging the person

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:18.798
that just saved your life.

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:19.566
Check.

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:25.638
Even if our want-to-be record
breakers do get to grips

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:28.341
with angular velocity,
hydrodynamic drag,

00:03:28.408 --> 00:03:34.781
and stability, like Tyler did,
186 feet is just too dangerous.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:39.852
Maybe it's better to kayak
somewhere safer, like a puddle.

